    },
    "body": [
     {
      "type": "TextualBody",
      "purpose": "describing",
      "value": "Prospect theory critiques expected-utility theory as a descriptive model: choices show pervasive violations, so value is assigned to GAINS and LOSSES relative to a reference point (not final assets), and probabilities are replaced by decision weights.",
      "language": "en"
     }
    ],
    "target": [
     {
      "type": "SpecificResource",
      "source": "https://shir-openu.github.io/meta-tagging-showcase/papers/kahneman-tversky1979-prospect.html",
      "selector": {
       "type": "TextQuoteSelector",
       "exact": "This paper presents a critique of expected utility theory as a descriptive model of decision making under risk, and develops an alternative model, called prospect theory",
       "prefix": "eplaced by decision weights. - \"",
       "suffix": "... value is assigned to gains a"
      }
     },
     {
      "type": "SpecificResource",
      "source": "https://shir-openu.github.io/meta-tagging-showcase/papers/kahneman-tversky1979-prospect.html",
      "selector": {
       "type": "TextQuoteSelector",
       "exact": "value is assigned to gains and losses rather than to final assets and in which probabilities are replaced by decision weights.",
       "prefix": "odel, called prospect theory... ",
       "suffix": "\" finding The value function is "
      }
     }
    ]
   },
